#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176717} :activity/id #uuid "2a069471-7d01-5a03-97db-297443dadb35" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049491 :topic/id #uuid "f90d8601-e439-5cfc-9a0b-afc8462d4f0a" :topic/math-academy-id 3632} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176716 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175977 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175827 :question/id #uuid "6a090caa-4c73-5f90-9cb1-dc7dcdfe9e37" :question/math-academy-id "e-8906"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f14ba254-fe70-511f-aad9-2903d4d6fe1e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262639} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364927}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Surface Area of Revolution"} :step/id #uuid "1ab1f1c7-2015-5a88-9ddc-d4b547073338" :step/math-academy-id 33266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176717 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176988 :tutorial/content "We've seen how to calculate the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis. We can also obtain surfaces of revolution by rotating a curve $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis.\n\nConsider the arc of the curve $x=f(y)$ that lies between the horizontal lines $y=y_1$ and $y=y_2.$ By rotating this curve $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis, we get a surface of revolution, as shown below on the right. How can we calculate its surface area?\n\n![](images/e6/e61f97b06c3228bb2671429d6562e97d6cdefefb7ac1acca31867eeb440a75ff.png)\n\nFirst, we divide the interval $[y_1,y_2]$ into small subintervals, each of length $\\Delta{y}.$ Let's consider a part of the curve that corresponds to one of those subintervals and let the length of the corresponding curved arc be $\\Delta{s}.$ When this part of the curve is rotated about the $y$-axis, it forms a closed strip. \n\n![](images/25/253837a6c151a74318b2e172e6db068a50d23c9cb93124588ae20a9379d9aa63.png)\n\nIf $\\Delta{y}$ is very small, we can assume that both left and right radii of the strip are approximately equal to $x=f(y).$ Then, this strip can be viewed as the lateral surface of a right cylinder with height $h=\\Delta{s}$ and radius $r=x=f(y).$ \n\n![](images/9f/9f10812beff0283e6029145b854a2faaa9b7a566751bd17514bc6817ef664dfc.png)\n\nWe can approximate the lateral surface area of such a cylinder as\n\n$$\nS_{\\text{strip}} \\approx S_{\\text{cylinder}} = 2\\pi r \\cdot h = 2\\pi x \\cdot \\Delta{s}.\n$$\n\nThe sum over all subintervals will approximate the total area $S$ that we want. The approximation becomes more and more accurate as $\\Delta{s} \\to 0$. Therefore, to get the area of the surface, we need to integrate:\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s\n$$\n\nRecall that for a curve $x = f(y),$ we can express the arc length element $\\textrm d s$ as\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d y}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can find the total surface area using the formula\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{y_1}^{y_2} x \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}y.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f1dd2211-511b-5b65-9250-ee6b84f0d566" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5319 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "76d50374-b9a8-5d13-90fe-031de2946fbe" :step/math-academy-id 33267 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176718}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176718 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175978 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175828 :question/id #uuid "6ee942e8-9fb9-5dfb-8f9c-aea1dd6d53ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-8907"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bff48a7c-66d6-55e8-99a1-ae8c80925335" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050393}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176233} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262634} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387692}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of Y"} :step/id #uuid "b8f345d7-4e41-5e19-b9f6-352cfd48cc6a" :step/math-academy-id 33268 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176763}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176742 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175992 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175843 :question/id #uuid "d0546997-c726-5ec2-ae10-c2373ed8b863" :question/math-academy-id "e-9121"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7cf4318c-a323-5224-92df-879ebc2bf3b0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364756} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387713}] :knowledge-point/title "Integrals Representing Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"} :step/id #uuid "29ad360c-8446-5b96-ba6b-4be86cac5c72" :step/math-academy-id 33994 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176716}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176763 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177009 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we're given a curve of the form $y=f(x)$ that lies between the vertical lines $x=x_1$ and $x=x_2.$ How do we calculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis?\n\nEarlier, we showed that the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nS = \\int 2\\pi x\\,\\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nFor a curve $y = f(x),$ the arc length element $\\textrm d s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the surface that is obtained when the $y=f(x)$ curve is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis is given by the formula\n\n$$\nS = 2\\pi \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} x \\sqrt{1+ \\left( \\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\right)^2} \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b0c21ed5-0845-50c6-ba7b-5e4581b700a8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5537 :tutorial/title "Surface Areas Generated by Functions of X"} :step/id #uuid "e55d411a-f21b-5007-bf69-39adfb956fe6" :step/math-academy-id 34386 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176742}}] :activity/title "Surface Areas of Revolution: Rotation About the Y-Axis" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176149}]}
